{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act just cause eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant only for statutory grounds, with required notice periods, and must provide relocation assistance for certain no-fault evictions. Self-help evictions are prohibited.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes, apartments, rooming/boarding homes. Exceptions: owner-occupied 2-3 unit premises with ≤2 rentals; transient hotel/motel guests; trust units for developmentally disabled family member.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Most residential rental properties; exceptions for owner-occupied 2-3 unit premises, transient guests, certain trust units."},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-unit premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel/motel guests, motel guests, guest houses rented to transient or seasonal tenant; unit held in trust for developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":" Listed below are the statutory grounds for eviction as set forth in the Anti-Eviction Statute.
A. Failure to Pay Rent
If a tenant fails to pay rent, the landlord may immediately take legal action to have the
tenant evicted.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey self-help eviction prohibition","requirement":"A landlord may not evict a tenant by self-help, such as changing locks, shutting off utilities, or removing belongings without a court judgment. Only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant for removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental premises in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental premises."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord or other person who illegally enters and/or holds a tenant’s belongings may be liable for damages to the tenant. In a successful action, the court may award possession and all damages, including court costs and reasonable attorney fees.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"“Self-help” eviction is entry into a dwelling unit and removal of tenants without their consent or without a judgment from a court, are not permitted in New Jersey under any circumstances.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
